Ladybird rolls back a logical property and its physical counterpart
together, using the element’s resolved writing mode and direction.
This restores logical UA margins under all: revert and keeps nested
revert-layer declarations in the same cascade search. The selected
source value retains its original shorthand interpretation before
being applied to the property that initiated the rollback.

Blink resolves logical surrogates before adding declarations to the
cascade map, through StyleCascade::ResolveSurrogate. Its ResolveRevert
and ResolveRevertLayer therefore search the combined property stack.
WebKit’s Builder::applyRollbackCascadeProperty asks the lower cascade
for lastPropertyResolvingLogicalPropertyPair, using the style’s
writing mode to select the last declaration of either paired property.
Gecko’s apply_properties maps logical IDs with to_physical before
apply_one_longhand records and checks the reverted cascade priorities.

The regression matches Chrome for UA paragraph and heading margins,
physical body margins, logical list padding, mixed declarations and
layer rollbacks across horizontal and vertical writing modes. It also
checks variable substitution and changes to inherited or own flow.
